@org=$A000
; Entity definition table: 128 definitions of 8 bytes
;
; Entity definitions for the moving things of a room: horizontal movers, vertical movers, the swinging rope and the
; flying dart (Willy is not one of them). A room lists up to 8 entities in its entity slots (#R$80F0, 2 bytes each:
; definition index, start data). The definition for index I is at $A000+8*I. The code at $8922-$8948 (in the room
; entry routine #R$88AE) copies each slot into the entity buffer (#R$8100): slot index bit 7 is dropped, the
; definition's bytes +0 and +1 are copied, the slot's second byte is written over the definition's byte +2 (so the
; table byte +2 is only a scratch byte, $00 in the image, rewritten at every room entry) and bytes +2 to +7 are
; copied after it. The entity routines #R$90C0 (moves) and #R$91BE (draws) then work on the buffer; both stop at an
; entry whose first byte is $FF.
; .
; Fields (buffer entry = definition with +2 taken from the slot). Types: 1 horizontal mover, 2 vertical mover,
; 3 rope, 4 dart; 0 = nothing (skipped); types 5-7 are not defined. Types 1 and 2 are drawn as 16x16 sprites with
; #R$9456, which tests collision against the background pixels: an overlap kills Willy.
; .
; +0 byte: bits 0-2 type (see above). The other bits are state that #R$90C0 changes. Type 1: bit 7 = direction (0
; left, 1 right), bits 5-6 = animation phase 0-3 (counted down when going left, up when going right); the entity
; steps one column every 4th pass, when the phase wraps (observed in the Bathroom). Type 2: bit 3 toggles every pass,
; bit 4 set = the phase advances every pass, clear = every second pass; bits 5-7 = animation phase 0-7 (observed
; with a poked entity: $02, $0A, $2A). Type 3: bit 7 = swing direction. Type 4: bit 7 = direction (0 left: byte +4
; decreases; 1 right: it increases). $FF ends the list.
; .
; +1 byte: types 1 and 2: bits 0-2 ink, bit 3 bright (the paper comes from the background), bits 5-7 = mask. The
; sprite is chosen as frame F = ((byte +0 AND byte +1) OR byte +2) >> 5 (bits 5-7), at page(+5)*256 + 32*F. The mask
; lets the animation phase (and the direction bit 7 for type 1) select the frame; mask bits that are 0 leave the frame
; to the slot's bits 5-7. Type 3 rope: swing position (index into #R$8300, bit 7 = side), changed by #R$90C0, which
; reverses the swing when the low 7 bits equal byte +7. Type 4: not read ($06).
; .
; +2 byte (the slot's second byte): types 1-3: bits 0-4 = column (0-31) of the sprite's left cell, bits 5-7 = frame
; offset OR-ed into the frame number (e.g. $8x selects frames 4-7). Type 4: Y position as an index into the row
; address table (#R$8200), so 2 * pixel row (bit 7 = lower half of the screen); the slot values are even except $D5
; for definition $45 in The Attic (room $29), which makes that dart read the table half a word off (inferred: its
; pixels then land in unused memory at $9Fxx, only attribute cells are changed).
; .
; +3 byte: types 1 and 2: Y position as an index into the row address table (2 * pixel row); #R$90C0 adds the step
; to it for type 2. Type 3: scratch (current column while the rope is drawn). Type 4: not read.
; .
; +4 byte: type 2: Y step added every pass (signed; $04 = 2 pixel rows down, $F4 = 6 rows up). Type 3: rope length in
; segments ($20). Type 4: X position in cells; moves one cell per pass (observed), shown while it is 0-31; a short
; beep (#R$91BE) is made when it reaches $2C (moving left) or $F4 (moving right), just before it enters the screen,
; so it repeats every 256 passes. Type 1: not read (the definitions hold $00 or $01: Unknown).
; .
; +5 byte: types 1 and 2: sprite page (high byte of the sprite address; pages $A6-$BF and $9C, see the sprite
; entries from #R$A600 and #R$9C40). Type 3 and 4: scratch (rope pixel mask; dart collision mask, $FF when the
; background cell has white ink, which is the ink of Willy).
; .
; +6 byte: type 1: leftmost column; type 2: top limit of Y; type 4: pixel pattern drawn above and below the solid
; centre line of the dart ($82 and $41 look like fins). Type 3: not read ($83).
; .
; +7 byte: type 1: rightmost column; type 2: bottom limit of Y; type 3: swing limit ($36); type 4: not read.
; .
; Limits: type 1 turns round when the column reaches byte +6 (going left) or +7 (going right). Type 2 reverses its
; step when Y reaches or passes byte +7, and when it reaches or passes byte +6 (then also clamped to +6).
; .
; The rope scratches the next entry's bytes +1 and +3 (its work variables are at +9 and +11 of the rope's own
; entry), so a rope is followed in the rooms by a dart or by the $FF terminator (rooms $10, $12, $19, $1F, $3A).
; .
; Indexes used by rooms $00-$3C: $01-$6F except $2B, plus $7F as the list terminator (slot index $FF, bit 7
; dropped). Not used: $00 (empty entity), $2B (a complete vertical mover with page $BA faces), $3F (a second
; $FF entry), $70-$7E (zeros). Rooms $3D-$3F do not hold valid room data. The comment on each line gives index, type,
; limits, sprite page and frames, and the rooms (hex) whose slots use it.
@label=EntityDefs
